I found two behaviour changes on `refactor/modules` and fixed both, keeping the rest of the refactor. The changes are uncommitted.

1. **`src/discount.js`, `applyDiscountCode`:** on `main`, an unknown discount code throws `unknown code: X`. The branch quietly returned 0 for it, so a mistyped code would give the customer no discount instead of an error. The error is back, and no code still means no discount.
2. **`src/lineItems.js`, `normalizeLineItem`:** when the validation moved into the new shared `assertInt` helper, the minimum quantity became `0` instead of `1`. That let lines with `qty: 0` through. It's now `assertInt(qty, 1, …)`.

Everything else I checked doesn't change what the code does:
- the tax, currency and discount tables have the same values;
- the renamed local variables;
- the switch from `function` to arrow functions;
- the money split, invoice and render code.

The existing tests missed both changes, so I added one test for each: `applyDiscountCode rejects an unknown code` and `normalizeLineItem rejects qty 0`. With the fixes all 519 tests pass. Without the fixes, only those 2 new tests fail.

One more difference, which I left alone: `src/money.js` no longer exists, so `require('./money')` breaks for anything outside this repo that uses it. Nothing in the repo uses it and the package is `private`, so I treated it as part of the refactor. If you want it kept, a 2-line `src/money.js` that re-exports `money/round` and `money/format` would do it.
